/T (Macro-economic Framework Since 2015, Bangladesh's economy has maintained a sustained pace of growth, with an average annual GDP increase of 5.5%. However, this uptrend did not translate into an increase in employment. Despite the growth, the number of people employed dropped by more than 9 million, thus mirroring a development model that continued to be based on the rise in productivity and the progressive replacement of labour with capital. )
/E (Macro-economic Framework Since 2015, Bangladesh's economy has maintained a sustained pace of growth, with an average annual GDP increase of 5.5%. However, this uptrend did not translate into an increase in employment. Despite the growth, the number of people employed dropped by more than 9 million, thus mirroring a development model that continued to be based on the rise in productivity and the progressive replacement of labour with capital. )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [5]
>>
